{"title":"Generate Parentheses","difficulty":"Medium","statement":"A string made only of the characters `(` and `)` is **balanced** (well-formed) if every `(` can be matched with a later `)`, and every `)` with an earlier `(`, so that the pairs nest properly. For example `(()())` and `()(())` are balanced, while `())(` and `(()` are not.\n\nFor each query value `n`, print **every** balanced string that uses exactly `n` pairs of parentheses (length `2n`), each exactly once, in ascending lexicographic order. In this ordering `(` comes before `)`.\n\nThe input holds several independent queries so that the output of each one stays on a single line.\n\n**Follow-up:** how many strings are there for a given `n`, and why does a plain 'try all 2^(2n) strings and filter' approach waste almost all of its work? Can you generate only valid strings?","inputFormat":"The first line contains an integer `T`, the number of queries.\nEach of the next `T` lines contains one integer `n`.","outputFormat":"For each query, print one line with all balanced strings for that `n`, separated by single spaces, in ascending lexicographic order.","constraints":["1 ≤ T ≤ 5","1 ≤ n ≤ 9"],"examples":[{"input":"1\n3","output":"((())) (()()) (())() ()(()) ()()()","explanation":"There are 5 balanced strings with 3 pairs. Sorted with '(' before ')', the strings starting with '((' come first, then those starting with '(()' ... and finally ()()()."},{"input":"2\n1\n2","output":"()\n(()) ()()","explanation":"One pair gives only (). Two pairs give (()) and ()(); (()) is smaller because its second character is '(' while ()() has ')' there."}],"tests":[{"input":"1\n1","output":"()"},{"input":"1\n2","output":"(()) ()()"},{"input":"1\n4","output":"(((()))) ((()())) ((())()) ((()))() (()(())) (()()()) (()())() (())(()) (())()() ()((())) ()(()()) ()(())() ()()(()) ()()()()"},{"input":"1\n5","output":"((((())))) (((()()))) (((())())) (((()))()) (((())))() ((()(()))) ((()()())) ((()())()) ((()()))() ((())(())) ((())(